ad-ed3fe8acc29d","content":"<p>Using an interactive format, theater students at Radford and Virginia Tech created &ldquo;Women and the Vote,&rdquo; a play about woman suffrage activists.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["69917baa-c605-47f9-90da-3addf2099e37"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence explains an advantage of the &ldquo;Women and the Vote&rdquo; format, noting that the format appealed to audiences because it allowed them to control the experience. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes a digital drawing on the &ldquo;Women and the Vote&rdquo; website; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the play&rsquo;s format. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence explains how audiences interacted with the &ldquo;Women and the Vote&rdquo; website; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the play&rsquo;s format. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that &ldquo;Women and the Vote&rdquo; had an interactive format, it doesn&rsquo;t explain what advantage this format might have.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-781-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ea11cf1a-b2af-4a55-8547-2fd4606e0133","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959352,"updateDate":1691007959352}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959352, 1691007959352),
    ($SATQ$94f48106$SATQ$, $SATQ$2ecd65e4-e5b9-4b95-a7c9-7f1f5031ccf3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In 2022, University of Miami researchers discovered brine pools in the Gulf of Aqaba.</li>
<li>A brine pool is an underwater lake that sits on the ocean floor.</li>
<li>The water in brine pools is three to eight times saltier than the surrounding ocean.</li>
<li>The extreme saltiness of this water makes it toxic to most sea life.</li>
<li>Some forms of bacteria are able to survive in brine pools.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to explain why brine pools are toxic to most sea life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3c816a56-a473-430c-82eb-5f1e08964ff4","content":"<p>Though brine pools are toxic to most sea life, some bacteria can survive there.</p>"},{"id":"45d98df6-c402-433f-9f4a-6dd0e4169a0d","content":"<p>The water in brine pools is toxic to most sea life because it is three to eight times saltier than the surrounding ocean.</p>"},{"id":"d82ed982-827e-4916-8114-1d641c346af4","content":"<p>The brine pools in the Gulf of Aqaba are toxic to most sea life and were discovered by researchers in 2022.</p>"},{"id":"df4f441c-dc21-4b4b-ad9a-7b309b6724e9","content":"<p>Brine pools are salty underwater lakes that sit on the ocean floor.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["45d98df6-c402-433f-9f4a-6dd0e4169a0d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence explains why brine pools are toxic to most sea life, noting that the water in the pools is three to eight times saltier than the surrounding ocean.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence states that brine pools are toxic to most sea life, it doesn&rsquo;t explain why the pools are toxic. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence states that brine pools are toxic to most sea life, it doesn&rsquo;t explain why the pools are toxic. Choice D is incorrect because the sentence describes brine pools, mentioning that they are salty, but doesn&rsquo;t explain why they are toxic to most sea life.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"94f48106","external_id":"2ecd65e4-e5b9-4b95-a7c9-7f1f5031ccf3","vaultid":null,"uId":"475519df-6de7-48db-94e0-3306cb0e8624","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In 2022, University of Miami researchers discovered brine pools in the Gulf of Aqaba.</li>\n<li>A brine pool is an underwater lake that sits on the ocean floor.</li>\n<li>The water in brine pools is three to eight times saltier than the surrounding ocean.</li>\n<li>The extreme saltiness of this water makes it toxic to most sea life.</li>\n<li>Some forms of bacteria are able to survive in brine pools.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain why brine pools are toxic to most sea life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3c816a56-a473-430c-82eb-5f1e08964ff4","content":"<p>Though brine pools are toxic to most sea life, some bacteria can survive there.</p>"},{"id":"45d98df6-c402-433f-9f4a-6dd0e4169a0d","content":"<p>The water in brine pools is toxic to most sea life because it is three to eight times saltier than the surrounding ocean.</p>"},{"id":"d82ed982-827e-4916-8114-1d641c346af4","content":"<p>The brine pools in the Gulf of Aqaba are toxic to most sea life and were discovered by researchers in 2022.</p>"},{"id":"df4f441c-dc21-4b4b-ad9a-7b309b6724e9","content":"<p>Brine pools are salty underwater lakes that sit on the ocean floor.</p>"}],"keys":["45d98df6-c402-433f-9f4a-6dd0e4169a0d"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence explains why brine pools are toxic to most sea life, noting that the water in the pools is three to eight times saltier than the surrounding ocean.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence states that brine pools are toxic to most sea life, it doesn&rsquo;t explain why the pools are toxic. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence states that brine pools are toxic to most sea life, it doesn&rsquo;t explain why the pools are toxic. Choice D is incorrect because the sentence describes brine pools, mentioning that they are salty, but doesn&rsquo;t explain why they are toxic to most sea life.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323034,"updateDate":1730147323034}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323034, 1730147323034),
    ($SATQ$94f4eecb$SATQ$, $SATQ$35c1814a-d776-4927-b0af-092bc597c446$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7de72ad6-d079-4723-ab7a-479c95d751a7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Las sergas de Esplandi&aacute;n</em> was a novel popular in sixteenth-century Spain.</li>
<li>The novel featured a fictional island inhabited solely by Black women and known as California.</li>
<li>That same century, Spanish explorers learned of an &ldquo;island&rdquo; off the west coast of Mexico.</li>
<li>They called it California after the island in the novel.</li>
<li>The &ldquo;island&rdquo; was actually the peninsula now known as Baja California (&ldquo;Lower California&rdquo;), which lies to the south of the US state of California.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the role a misconception played in the naming of a place. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9b395dcd-5391-4844-8bd3-d65658ce32de","content":"<p>The novel <em>Las sergas de Esplandi&aacute;n </em>featured a fictional island known as California.</p>"},{"id":"5de66df7-a971-46db-8981-f0f6af5ff668","content":"<p>To the south of the US state of California lies Baja California (&ldquo;Lower California&rdquo;), originally called California after a fictional place.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"bdfa5f93-3a77-41d4-8fc9-fc720ce702be","content":"<p>In the sixteenth century, Spanish explorers learned of a peninsula off the west coast of Mexico and called it California.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"34244eaa-17b2-4dfc-bc34-4862f8e21676","content":"<p>Thinking it was an island, Spanish explorers called a peninsula California after an island in a popular novel.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["34244eaa-17b2-4dfc-bc34-4862f8e21676"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the role a misconception played in the naming of a place, explaining that Spanish explorers mistook a peninsula for an island and, as a result, named the peninsula after a fictional island, California. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence mentions a novel that featured a fictional island, California; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the role a misconception played in the naming of a place. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence notes that Baja California was originally named after a fictional place; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the role a misconception&mdash;specifically, the Spanish explorers&rsquo; mistaken belief that the peninsula was an island&mdash;played in the naming of a place. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates when Spanish explorers learned of the peninsula they called California; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the role a misconception played in the naming of a place. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-786-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$81c4c5d4-7454-4f09-a3c2-5bd097825786$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"94f4eecb","external_id":"35c1814a-d776-4927-b0af-092bc597c446","vaultid":"7de72ad6-d079-4723-ab7a-479c95d751a7","uId":"e8458a40-0554-4ac9-9d74-d2864439052d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Las sergas de Esplandi&aacute;n</em> was a novel popular in sixteenth-century Spain.</li>\n<li>The novel featured a fictional island inhabited solely by Black women and known as California.</li>\n<li>That same century, Spanish explorers learned of an &ldquo;island&rdquo; off the west coast of Mexico.</li>\n<li>They called it California after the island in the novel.</li>\n<li>The &ldquo;island&rdquo; was actually the peninsula now known as Baja California (&ldquo;Lower California&rdquo;), which lies to the south of the US state of California.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the role a misconception played in the naming of a place. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9b395dcd-5391-4844-8bd3-d65658ce32de","content":"<p>The novel <em>Las sergas de Esplandi&aacute;n </em>featured a fictional island known as California.</p>"},{"id":"5de66df7-a971-46db-8981-f0f6af5ff668","content":"<p>To the south of the US state of California lies Baja California (&ldquo;Lower California&rdquo;), originally called California after a fictional place.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"bdfa5f93-3a77-41d4-8fc9-fc720ce702be","content":"<p>In the sixteenth century, Spanish explorers learned of a peninsula off the west coast of Mexico and called it California.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"34244eaa-17b2-4dfc-bc34-4862f8e21676","content":"<p>Thinking it was an island, Spanish explorers called a peninsula California after an island in a popular novel.</p>"}],"keys":["34244eaa-17b2-4dfc-bc34-4862f8e21676"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the role a misconception played in the naming of a place, explaining that Spanish explorers mistook a peninsula for an island and, as a result, named the peninsula after a fictional island, California. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence mentions a novel that featured a fictional island, California; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the role a misconception played in the naming of a place. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence notes that Baja California was originally named after a fictional place; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the role a misconception&mdash;specifically, the Spanish explorers&rsquo; mistaken belief that the peninsula was an island&mdash;played in the naming of a place. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates when Spanish explorers learned of the peninsula they called California; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the role a misconception played in the naming of a place. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-786-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"81c4c5d4-7454-4f09-a3c2-5bd097825786","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$9502ec65$SATQ$, $SATQ$95351918-250d-4833-a66c-453eebf233c6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$c421de6b-15cf-4362-ab43-592ab62ea74d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When soil becomes contaminated by toxic metals, it can be removed from the ground and disposed of in a landfill. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> contaminated soil can be detoxified via phytoremediation: plants that can withstand high concentrations of metals absorb the pollutants and store them in their shoots, which are then cut off and safely disposed of, preserving the health of the plants.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"722c1cc3-234e-4c80-90cd-37752ce698f1","content":"<p>Alternatively,</p>"},{"id":"0d9405cb-3ecd-4ff3-ae7f-a6ef2e4cc668","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"db23497f-6c04-4951-be24-00afec972c4c","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"9987f318-0279-459a-805f-d35ec59a44b3","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["722c1cc3-234e-4c80-90cd-37752ce698f1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Alternatively&rdquo; logically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence&mdash;removing toxic metals from the soil via phytoremediation&mdash;offers an alternative to the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence specifies or elaborates on an aspect of the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). Instead, phytoremediation is an alternative to that method. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence is an example of the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). Instead, phytoremediation is an alternative to that method. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence is a result or consequence of the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). Instead, phytoremediation is an alternative to that method. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-217-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9b024c9b-0c2e-47fd-afd1-fe8f96138004$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9502ec65","external_id":"95351918-250d-4833-a66c-453eebf233c6","vaultid":"c421de6b-15cf-4362-ab43-592ab62ea74d","uId":"602c1bf4-205e-4358-aea3-2dc91e7541f2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When soil becomes contaminated by toxic metals, it can be removed from the ground and disposed of in a landfill. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> contaminated soil can be detoxified via phytoremediation: plants that can withstand high concentrations of metals absorb the pollutants and store them in their shoots, which are then cut off and safely disposed of, preserving the health of the plants.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"722c1cc3-234e-4c80-90cd-37752ce698f1","content":"<p>Alternatively,</p>"},{"id":"0d9405cb-3ecd-4ff3-ae7f-a6ef2e4cc668","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"db23497f-6c04-4951-be24-00afec972c4c","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"9987f318-0279-459a-805f-d35ec59a44b3","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"}],"keys":["722c1cc3-234e-4c80-90cd-37752ce698f1"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Alternatively&rdquo; logically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence&mdash;removing toxic metals from the soil via phytoremediation&mdash;offers an alternative to the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence specifies or elaborates on an aspect of the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). Instead, phytoremediation is an alternative to that method. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence is an example of the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). Instead, phytoremediation is an alternative to that method. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the soil decontamination method described in this sentence is a result or consequence of the previously described method (removing the contaminated soil from the ground). Instead, phytoremediation is an alternative to that method. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-217-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9b024c9b-0c2e-47fd-afd1-fe8f96138004","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959327,"updateDate":1691007959327}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959327, 1691007959327),
    ($SATQ$9551ef8b$SATQ$, $SATQ$c93c8f6e-0b3e-4c5a-b751-a2d724dec205$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$71eff558-601d-4b7b-8fcf-950b1b649ce6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The magnificent frigatebird (<em>fregata magnificens</em>) is a species of seabird that feeds mainly on fish, tuna, squid, and other small sea animals.</li>
<li>It is unusual among seabirds in that it doesn&rsquo;t dive into the water for prey.</li>
<li>One way it acquires food is by using its hook-tipped bill to snatch prey from the surface of the water.</li>
<li>Another way it acquires food is by taking it from weaker birds by force.</li>
<li>This behavior is known as kleptoparasitism.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two ways a magnificent frigatebird acquires food. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b29f04dc-1e52-46bd-ab8b-b4408caf0630","content":"<p>A magnificent frigatebird never dives into the water, instead using its hook-tipped bill to snatch prey from the surface.</p>"},{"id":"f14a3818-dd34-4abb-b8fe-e43bfc1c8b6f","content":"<p>Neither of a magnificent frigatebird&rsquo;s two ways of acquiring food requires the bird to dive into the water.</p>"},{"id":"3a2c3628-04b0-4984-a0a6-83bef2d3acf8","content":"<p>Of the magnificent frigatebird&rsquo;s two ways of acquiring food, only one is known as kleptoparasitism.</p>"},{"id":"4332ce17-5939-4445-a5b1-0c5e51837be0","content":"<p>In addition to snatching prey from the water with its hook-tipped bill, a magnificent frigatebird takes food from other birds by force.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f14a3818-dd34-4abb-b8fe-e43bfc1c8b6f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the two ways a magnificent frigatebird acquires food, noting that neither way requires the seabird to dive into the water. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes how a magnificent frigatebird captures prey without diving into water; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two ways the seabird acquires food. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence notes the term used to describe one of the two ways that magnificent frigatebirds acquire food; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two ways. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence describes the two ways that a magnificent frigatebird acquires food; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two ways. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-783-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b2c6922c-1cc1-452e-aa92-cc1f112dd36e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9551ef8b","external_id":"c93c8f6e-0b3e-4c5a-b751-a2d724dec205","vaultid":"71eff558-601d-4b7b-8fcf-950b1b649ce6","uId":"5be85821-a40b-42c8-9da3-cb3b85376799","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The magnificent frigatebird (<em>fregata magnificens</em>) is a species of seabird that feeds mainly on fish, tuna, squid, and other small sea animals.</li>\n<li>It is unusual among seabirds in that it doesn&rsquo;t dive into the water for prey.</li>\n<li>One way it acquires food is by using its hook-tipped bill to snatch prey from the surface of the water.</li>\n<li>Another way it acquires food is by taking it from weaker birds by force.</li>\n<li>This behavior is known as kleptoparasitism.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two ways a magnificent frigatebird acquires food. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b29f04dc-1e52-46bd-ab8b-b4408caf0630","content":"<p>A magnificent frigatebird never dives into the water, instead using its hook-tipped bill to snatch prey from the surface.</p>"},{"id":"f14a3818-dd34-4abb-b8fe-e43bfc1c8b6f","content":"<p>Neither of a magnificent frigatebird&rsquo;s two ways of acquiring food requires the bird to dive into the water.</p>"},{"id":"3a2c3628-04b0-4984-a0a6-83bef2d3acf8","content":"<p>Of the magnificent frigatebird&rsquo;s two ways of acquiring food, only one is known as kleptoparasitism.</p>"},{"id":"4332ce17-5939-4445-a5b1-0c5e51837be0","content":"<p>In addition to snatching prey from the water with its hook-tipped bill, a magnificent frigatebird takes food from other birds by force.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["f14a3818-dd34-4abb-b8fe-e43bfc1c8b6f"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the two ways a magnificent frigatebird acquires food, noting that neither way requires the seabird to dive into the water. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes how a magnificent frigatebird captures prey without diving into water; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two ways the seabird acquires food. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence notes the term used to describe one of the two ways that magnificent frigatebirds acquire food; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two ways. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence describes the two ways that a magnificent frigatebird acquires food; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two ways. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-783-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b2c6922c-1cc1-452e-aa92-cc1f112dd36e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$964c6055$SATQ$, $SATQ$abe6747a-221f-4f61-bcd7-d10dd928c01b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Two opposing theories of vision divided scholars for many centuries.</li>
<li>The ancient Greek mathematician Euclid (circa 300 BCE) supported the extramission theory.</li>
<li>This theory held that the eyes emit a form of radiation that illuminates objects in its range.</li>
<li>The ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle (384&ndash;322 BCE) supported the intromission theory.</li>
<li>This theory held that objects emit a form of radiation that reaches the eyes.</li>
<li>In the eleventh century, Arab mathematician Ibn al-Haytham (965&ndash;1040 CE) largely settled the debate with the first conclusive experiments supporting intromission.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to provide a historical overview of the two theories. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2b548159-6f13-47cd-b0ff-1c6a2f467610","content":"<p>Scholars were divided between the extramission and intromission theories of vision until Ibn al-Haytham&rsquo;s eleventh-century experiments largely settled the debate in support of intromission.</p>"},{"id":"98b8ea30-1fec-46c9-8a4f-f5e6bae9fac3","content":"<p>Through two opposing theories of vision&mdash;extramission and intromission&mdash;Euclid, Aristotle, and Ibn al-Haytham held that a form of radiation is emitted either from objects or from the eyes.</p>"},{"id":"1c17fb24-625a-4166-beeb-6a55fa3eca6d","content":"<p>While Ibn al-Haytham largely settled the debate in the eleventh century, Aristotle supported the theory of intromission centuries before.</p>"},{"id":"4d42ed64-d97d-477e-8d78-3a48c46251e1","content":"<p>Before the eleventh century, the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle supported the intromission theory, which held that objects emit a form of radiation that reaches the eyes.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2b548159-6f13-47cd-b0ff-1c6a2f467610"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence provides a historical overview of the two theories, noting that scholars were divided between them before Ibn al-Haytham&rsquo;s experiments supporting intromission largely settled the debate in the eleventh century.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence identifies the two theories and the scholars that supported them, it doesn&rsquo;t provide a historical overview of the theories. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence indicates Aristotle supported intromission theory centuries before Ibn al-Haytham settled the debate, it doesn&rsquo;t mention extramission theory or provide a historical overview of both theories. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence provides information about intromission theory but doesn&rsquo;t mention extramission theory or provide a historical overview of both theories.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"964c6055","external_id":"abe6747a-221f-4f61-bcd7-d10dd928c01b","vaultid":null,"uId":"364f71da-6b63-47f1-aae9-7814efaa08a8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two opposing theories of vision divided scholars for many centuries.</li>\n<li>The ancient Greek mathematician Euclid (circa 300 BCE) supported the extramission theory.</li>\n<li>This theory held that the eyes emit a form of radiation that illuminates objects in its range.</li>\n<li>The ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle (384&ndash;322 BCE) supported the intromission theory.</li>\n<li>This theory held that objects emit a form of radiation that reaches the eyes.</li>\n<li>In the eleventh century, Arab mathematician Ibn al-Haytham (965&ndash;1040 CE) largely settled the debate with the first conclusive experiments supporting intromission.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to provide a historical overview of the two theories. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2b548159-6f13-47cd-b0ff-1c6a2f467610","content":"<p>Scholars were divided between the extramission and intromission theories of vision until Ibn al-Haytham&rsquo;s eleventh-century experiments largely settled the debate in support of intromission.</p>"},{"id":"98b8ea30-1fec-46c9-8a4f-f5e6bae9fac3","content":"<p>Through two opposing theories of vision&mdash;extramission and intromission&mdash;Euclid, Aristotle, and Ibn al-Haytham held that a form of radiation is emitted either from objects or from the eyes.</p>"},{"id":"1c17fb24-625a-4166-beeb-6a55fa3eca6d","content":"<p>While Ibn al-Haytham largely settled the debate in the eleventh century, Aristotle supported the theory of intromission centuries before.</p>"},{"id":"4d42ed64-d97d-477e-8d78-3a48c46251e1","content":"<p>Before the eleventh century, the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle supported the intromission theory, which held that objects emit a form of radiation that reaches the eyes.</p>"}],"keys":["2b548159-6f13-47cd-b0ff-1c6a2f467610"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence provides a historical overview of the two theories, noting that scholars were divided between them before Ibn al-Haytham&rsquo;s experiments supporting intromission largely settled the debate in the eleventh century.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence identifies the two theories and the scholars that supported them, it doesn&rsquo;t provide a historical overview of the theories. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence indicates Aristotle supported intromission theory centuries before Ibn al-Haytham settled the debate, it doesn&rsquo;t mention extramission theory or provide a historical overview of both theories. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence provides information about intromission theory but doesn&rsquo;t mention extramission theory or provide a historical overview of both theories.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061190,"updateDate":1755117061190}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061190, 1755117061190),
    ($SATQ$96a86bce$SATQ$, $SATQ$84f5f227-03be-40cf-addc-599ce1a63da9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2468c4b3-0394-4691-a53d-188cb7cbff03$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cambodia&rsquo;s Angkor Wat was built in the 1100s to honor the Hindu god Vishnu.</li>
<li>It has been a Buddhist temple since the sixteenth century.</li>
<li>Decorrelation stretch analysis is a novel digital imaging technique that enhances the contrast between colors in a photograph.</li>
<li>Archaeologist Noel Hidalgo Tan applied decorrelation stretch analysis to photographs he had taken of Angkor Wat&rsquo;s plaster walls.</li>
<li>Tan&rsquo;s analysis revealed hundreds of images unknown to researchers.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to present Tan&rsquo;s research to an audience unfamiliar with Angkor Wat. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"24a7c23f-bf67-4278-8bfb-b13ce1761697","content":"<p>Tan photographed Angkor Wat&rsquo;s plaster walls and then applied decorrelation stretch analysis to the photographs.</p>"},{"id":"92f7661a-04db-4308-8513-e029088ce136","content":"<p>Decorrelation stretch analysis is a novel digital imaging technique that Tan used to enhance the contrast between colors in a photograph.</p>"},{"id":"7c3cf7f5-5a41-4699-81f0-4859ae96f90a","content":"<p>Using a novel digital imaging technique, Tan revealed hundreds of images hidden on the walls of Angkor Wat, a Cambodian temple.</p>"},{"id":"a96aa85e-0158-40d8-b974-470844c981cd","content":"<p>Built to honor a Hindu god before becoming a Buddhist temple, Cambodia&rsquo;s Angkor Wat concealed hundreds of images on its plaster walls.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7c3cf7f5-5a41-4699-81f0-4859ae96f90a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively presents Tan&rsquo;s research to an audience unfamiliar with Angkor Wat, explaining the results of the research and identifying Angkor Wat as a temple in Cambodia. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence presents Tan&rsquo;s research, it fails to explain what Angkor Wat is for an audience unfamiliar with the temple. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the role that decorrelation stretch analysis played in Tan&rsquo;s research; it doesn&rsquo;t present the research, which would require specifying where it was conducted. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence explains what Angkor Wat is, it fails to present Tan&rsquo;s research. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-729-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4faf9b56-43ee-4d1d-828d-aa8f779d0221$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"96a86bce","external_id":"84f5f227-03be-40cf-addc-599ce1a63da9","vaultid":"2468c4b3-0394-4691-a53d-188cb7cbff03","uId":"a808dd1a-78f6-4f37-bc45-22c3ef9cd5bd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cambodia&rsquo;s Angkor Wat was built in the 1100s to honor the Hindu god Vishnu.</li>\n<li>It has been a Buddhist temple since the sixteenth century.</li>\n<li>Decorrelation stretch analysis is a novel digital imaging technique that enhances the contrast between colors in a photograph.</li>\n<li>Archaeologist Noel Hidalgo Tan applied decorrelation stretch analysis to photographs he had taken of Angkor Wat&rsquo;s plaster walls.</li>\n<li>Tan&rsquo;s analysis revealed hundreds of images unknown to researchers.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to present Tan&rsquo;s research to an audience unfamiliar with Angkor Wat. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"24a7c23f-bf67-4278-8bfb-b13ce1761697","content":"<p>Tan photographed Angkor Wat&rsquo;s plaster walls and then applied decorrelation stretch analysis to the photographs.</p>"},{"id":"92f7661a-04db-4308-8513-e029088ce136","content":"<p>Decorrelation stretch analysis is a novel digital imaging technique that Tan used to enhance the contrast between colors in a photograph.</p>"},{"id":"7c3cf7f5-5a41-4699-81f0-4859ae96f90a","content":"<p>Using a novel digital imaging technique, Tan revealed hundreds of images hidden on the walls of Angkor Wat, a Cambodian temple.</p>"},{"id":"a96aa85e-0158-40d8-b974-470844c981cd","content":"<p>Built to honor a Hindu god before becoming a Buddhist temple, Cambodia&rsquo;s Angkor Wat concealed hundreds of images on its plaster walls.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["7c3cf7f5-5a41-4699-81f0-4859ae96f90a"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively presents Tan&rsquo;s research to an audience unfamiliar with Angkor Wat, explaining the results of the research and identifying Angkor Wat as a temple in Cambodia. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence presents Tan&rsquo;s research, it fails to explain what Angkor Wat is for an audience unfamiliar with the temple. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the role that decorrelation stretch analysis played in Tan&rsquo;s research; it doesn&rsquo;t present the research, which would require specifying where it was conducted. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence explains what Angkor Wat is, it fails to present Tan&rsquo;s research. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-729-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4faf9b56-43ee-4d1d-828d-aa8f779d0221","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$973632d2$SATQ$, $SATQ$5cc70221-e8d0-4917-95ba-4d9ec35d842b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$27425e12-803e-43d8-988a-4ba45dadf429$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In North America, woodlands have expanded into areas that were once grasslands.</li>
<li>Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell of Wichita State University investigated whether woodland expansion is related to changes in climate.</li>
<li>Rogers and Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past and indexed the age of the trees with historical climate data to see if tree populations and climate were correlated.</li>
<li>Tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.</li>
<li>Droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ec9d6f1e-a7af-4339-a7c9-77b2cdd6aca3","content":"<p>Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, researchers at Wichita State University, wanted to know if woodland expansion is related to changes in climate.</p>"},{"id":"e731a772-5605-40aa-aede-44f14baf84bd","content":"<p>Thanks to the work done by Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, we now know that droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.</p>"},{"id":"05ac39ff-5d80-4d4f-b902-b0f362e54bdc","content":"<p>Wichita State University researchers have determined that tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.</p>"},{"id":"7e19ddcc-13c6-4c5a-86f4-5c378ba01b1e","content":"<p>Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past, indexing the age of the trees with historical climate data.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ec9d6f1e-a7af-4339-a7c9-77b2cdd6aca3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence effectively emphasizes the aim, or goal, of the research study (in other words, what the researchers hoped to learn from the study): Rogers and Russell wanted to know if woodland expansion is related to changes in climate. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the researchers&rsquo; findings; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the aim of the study. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the results of the study; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the aim. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the methodology of the study; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the aim. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-724-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0ba0bd8b-ff6a-420f-9005-051771792fd1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"973632d2","external_id":"5cc70221-e8d0-4917-95ba-4d9ec35d842b","vaultid":"27425e12-803e-43d8-988a-4ba45dadf429","uId":"a9cba9c3-a755-4f6f-9a16-0d520ad9154d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In North America, woodlands have expanded into areas that were once grasslands.</li>\n<li>Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell of Wichita State University investigated whether woodland expansion is related to changes in climate.</li>\n<li>Rogers and Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past and indexed the age of the trees with historical climate data to see if tree populations and climate were correlated.</li>\n<li>Tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.</li>\n<li>Droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ec9d6f1e-a7af-4339-a7c9-77b2cdd6aca3","content":"<p>Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, researchers at Wichita State University, wanted to know if woodland expansion is related to changes in climate.</p>"},{"id":"e731a772-5605-40aa-aede-44f14baf84bd","content":"<p>Thanks to the work done by Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, we now know that droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.</p>"},{"id":"05ac39ff-5d80-4d4f-b902-b0f362e54bdc","content":"<p>Wichita State University researchers have determined that tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.</p>"},{"id":"7e19ddcc-13c6-4c5a-86f4-5c378ba01b1e","content":"<p>Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past, indexing the age of the trees with historical climate data.</p>"}],"keys":["ec9d6f1e-a7af-4339-a7c9-77b2cdd6aca3"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence effectively emphasizes the aim, or goal, of the research study (in other words, what the researchers hoped to learn from the study): Rogers and Russell wanted to know if woodland expansion is related to changes in climate. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the researchers&rsquo; findings; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the aim of the study. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the results of the study; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the aim. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the methodology of the study; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the aim. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-724-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0ba0bd8b-ff6a-420f-9005-051771792fd1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$974b5a8c$SATQ$, $SATQ$c8593e14-0f20-473c-803a-5ea66e1777a3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Madison is a type of line dance that involves neat rows of dancers performing a repeated sequence of steps in unison. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> many other dances are also defined by order, repetition, and synchronicity, but the Madison is distinguished by its extreme uniformity; when an auditorium full of dancers performs the Madison, one almost gets the impression of a military march.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2b439358-30d0-4f11-9572-100d600c26cb","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"f7742f6d-619f-453f-abd5-8705a8501ecb","content":"<p>Of course,</p>"},{"id":"bd3de9ca-aff1-43ac-81b8-f509c14fe8b6","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"093b643e-0a4e-486d-840b-c50752c610e9","content":"<p>Moreover,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f7742f6d-619f-453f-abd5-8705a8501ecb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "Of course" logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;about many dances being "defined by order, repetition, and synchronicity"&mdash;acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the previous description of the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "however" illogically signals that the information that follows contrasts with the previous description of the Madison. Instead, the information about many dances&rsquo; features acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison. Choice C is incorrect because "specifically" illogically signals that the information that follows specifies or elaborates on the previous description of the Madison. Instead, the information about many dances&rsquo; features acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison. Choice D is incorrect because "moreover" illogically signals that the information that follows adds to or expands on the previous description of the Madison. Instead, the information about many dances&rsquo; features acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"974b5a8c","external_id":"c8593e14-0f20-473c-803a-5ea66e1777a3","vaultid":null,"uId":"94b09eb2-7479-4274-9f9b-13c4ae8ced03","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Madison is a type of line dance that involves neat rows of dancers performing a repeated sequence of steps in unison. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> many other dances are also defined by order, repetition, and synchronicity, but the Madison is distinguished by its extreme uniformity; when an auditorium full of dancers performs the Madison, one almost gets the impression of a military march.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2b439358-30d0-4f11-9572-100d600c26cb","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"f7742f6d-619f-453f-abd5-8705a8501ecb","content":"<p>Of course,</p>"},{"id":"bd3de9ca-aff1-43ac-81b8-f509c14fe8b6","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"093b643e-0a4e-486d-840b-c50752c610e9","content":"<p>Moreover,</p>"}],"keys":["f7742f6d-619f-453f-abd5-8705a8501ecb"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"Of course\" logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;about many dances being \"defined by order, repetition, and synchronicity\"&mdash;acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the previous description of the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"however\" illogically signals that the information that follows contrasts with the previous description of the Madison. Instead, the information about many dances&rsquo; features acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison. Choice C is incorrect because \"specifically\" illogically signals that the information that follows specifies or elaborates on the previous description of the Madison. Instead, the information about many dances&rsquo; features acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison. Choice D is incorrect because \"moreover\" illogically signals that the information that follows adds to or expands on the previous description of the Madison. Instead, the information about many dances&rsquo; features acknowledges an obvious similarity between these dances and the Madison. The sentence then goes on to emphasize a distinguishing feature of the Madison.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061191,"updateDate":1755117061191}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061191, 1755117061191),
    ($SATQ$97e2e364$SATQ$, $SATQ$44767664-ffb2-41bc-b684-ce10c1311923$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$07bbb58c-e3c8-4ef3-9a80-fe179f4e4b9c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Okot p&rsquo;Bitek&rsquo;s poem <em>Song of Lawino</em> (1966) explores postcolonial Ugandan life through the eyes of a woman living in a rural village. With its vibrant imagery, bitingly satiric tone, and dexterous use of traditional Acholi song and phraseology, the poem inspired a generation of East African writers. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> those who adopted its style are often referred to as Okot School poets. &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3c7044e6-faa9-47ae-8fd6-929600117296","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"18a83e15-b5cf-41e9-ad43-5ff5adbed457","content":"<p>Fittingly,</p>"},{"id":"8f139feb-f680-4e32-a41d-69da29382bd0","content":"<p>By comparison,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"67a0edaa-2a66-4800-bbfe-03cbe10c705c","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["18a83e15-b5cf-41e9-ad43-5ff5adbed457"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Fittingly&rdquo; is a transition that means &ldquo;appropriately&rdquo; or &ldquo;suitably,&rdquo; and it is appropriate that writers who adopted their style from Okot p&rsquo;Bitek would be known as the Okot School poets. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence is not disagreeing with anything&mdash;rather, it&rsquo;s discussing how it makes sense for those writers who adopted Okot&rsquo;s style to be known as the Okot School poets. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that compares two ideas. But this sentence is not comparing the Okot School poets and their style to Okot&rsquo;s style. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence is not disagreeing with anything&mdash;rather, it&rsquo;s discussing how it makes sense for those writers who adopted Okot&rsquo;s style to be known as the Okot School poets. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-M006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$384fda20-c6a1-4bfe-840e-10b06184c037$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"97e2e364","external_id":"44767664-ffb2-41bc-b684-ce10c1311923","vaultid":"07bbb58c-e3c8-4ef3-9a80-fe179f4e4b9c","uId":"219f2178-16f8-4d18-81d2-03915e126221","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Okot p&rsquo;Bitek&rsquo;s poem <em>Song of Lawino</em> (1966) explores postcolonial Ugandan life through the eyes of a woman living in a rural village. With its vibrant imagery, bitingly satiric tone, and dexterous use of traditional Acholi song and phraseology, the poem inspired a generation of East African writers. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> those who adopted its style are often referred to as Okot School poets. &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3c7044e6-faa9-47ae-8fd6-929600117296","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"18a83e15-b5cf-41e9-ad43-5ff5adbed457","content":"<p>Fittingly,</p>"},{"id":"8f139feb-f680-4e32-a41d-69da29382bd0","content":"<p>By comparison,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"67a0edaa-2a66-4800-bbfe-03cbe10c705c","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"}],"keys":["18a83e15-b5cf-41e9-ad43-5ff5adbed457"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Fittingly&rdquo; is a transition that means &ldquo;appropriately&rdquo; or &ldquo;suitably,&rdquo; and it is appropriate that writers who adopted their style from Okot p&rsquo;Bitek would be known as the Okot School poets. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence is not disagreeing with anything&mdash;rather, it&rsquo;s discussing how it makes sense for those writers who adopted Okot&rsquo;s style to be known as the Okot School poets. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that compares two ideas. But this sentence is not comparing the Okot School poets and their style to Okot&rsquo;s style. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence is not disagreeing with anything&mdash;rather, it&rsquo;s discussing how it makes sense for those writers who adopted Okot&rsquo;s style to be known as the Okot School poets. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-M006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"384fda20-c6a1-4bfe-840e-10b06184c037","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959373,"updateDate":1691007959373}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959373, 1691007959373),
    ($SATQ$99183985$SATQ$, $SATQ$f8ac7e78-a209-4437-a3de-2899ad555b36$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0031bc2f-18b1-452d-a64e-7544176dee17$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Some sandstone arches in Utah&rsquo;s Arches National Park have been defaced by tourists&rsquo; carvings.</li>
<li>Park rangers can smooth away some carvings using power grinders.</li>
<li>For deep carvings, power grinding is not always feasible because it can greatly alter or damage the rock.</li>
<li>Park rangers can use an infilling technique, which involves filling in carvings with ground sandstone and a bonding agent.</li>
<li>This technique is minimally invasive.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of the infilling technique. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"58c7654e-531c-402f-8bc4-d4358a29c391","content":"<p>To remove carvings from sandstone arches in Utah&rsquo;s Arches National Park, power grinding is not always feasible.</p>"},{"id":"c345d064-0fbf-41df-bb32-937c1466ad17","content":"<p>Filling in carvings with ground sandstone and a bonding agent is less invasive than smoothing them away with a power grinder, which can greatly alter or damage the sandstone arches.</p>"},{"id":"daf82e45-4a13-40ab-82b8-d341eb772602","content":"<p>Park rangers can use a power grinding technique to smooth away carvings or fill them in with ground sandstone and a bonding agent.</p>"},{"id":"f574e2e7-eb79-4f6e-ad23-12604fc64ba0","content":"<p>As methods for removing carvings from sandstone, power grinding and infilling differ in their level of invasiveness.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c345d064-0fbf-41df-bb32-937c1466ad17"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence effectively explains an advantage of infilling: it&rsquo;s less invasive than using a power grinder. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence identifies a disadvantage of power grinding; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of infilling. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence identifies the two techniques park rangers use; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of infilling. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence indicates that power grinding and infilling are different in one aspect; it fails to explain an advantage of infilling. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-738-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$ebbdab6c-9bb1-4d3c-8546-2c6258ef8a79$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"99183985","external_id":"f8ac7e78-a209-4437-a3de-2899ad555b36","vaultid":"0031bc2f-18b1-452d-a64e-7544176dee17","uId":"ea45ea7e-63f5-46aa-9643-8ac0fa7dcd50","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some sandstone arches in Utah&rsquo;s Arches National Park have been defaced by tourists&rsquo; carvings.</li>\n<li>Park rangers can smooth away some carvings using power grinders.</li>\n<li>For deep carvings, power grinding is not always feasible because it can greatly alter or damage the rock.</li>\n<li>Park rangers can use an infilling technique, which involves filling in carvings with ground sandstone and a bonding agent.</li>\n<li>This technique is minimally invasive.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of the infilling technique. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"58c7654e-531c-402f-8bc4-d4358a29c391","content":"<p>To remove carvings from sandstone arches in Utah&rsquo;s Arches National Park, power grinding is not always feasible.</p>"},{"id":"c345d064-0fbf-41df-bb32-937c1466ad17","content":"<p>Filling in carvings with ground sandstone and a bonding agent is less invasive than smoothing them away with a power grinder, which can greatly alter or damage the sandstone arches.</p>"},{"id":"daf82e45-4a13-40ab-82b8-d341eb772602","content":"<p>Park rangers can use a power grinding technique to smooth away carvings or fill them in with ground sandstone and a bonding agent.</p>"},{"id":"f574e2e7-eb79-4f6e-ad23-12604fc64ba0","content":"<p>As methods for removing carvings from sandstone, power grinding and infilling differ in their level of invasiveness.</p>"}],"keys":["c345d064-0fbf-41df-bb32-937c1466ad17"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence effectively explains an advantage of infilling: it&rsquo;s less invasive than using a power grinder. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence identifies a disadvantage of power grinding; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of infilling. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence identifies the two techniques park rangers use; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of infilling. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence indicates that power grinding and infilling are different in one aspect; it fails to explain an advantage of infilling. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-738-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ebbdab6c-9bb1-4d3c-8546-2c6258ef8a79","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$9922b364$SATQ$, $SATQ$37bee52c-3d2d-4773-bc56-009278a1efa6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ibn Sina was a Persian philosopher and physician.</li>
<li>His book <em>The Canon of Medicine</em> recorded the most advanced medical knowledge of his time.</li>
<li>It was published in the year <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1025</span> CE.</li>
<li>It was used as a medical textbook in Middle Eastern and European universities for centuries.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to identify the year that <em>The Canon of Medicine</em> was published. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"886d631c-57af-4501-bf65-5b49e066ebcb","content":"<p>Ibn Sina&rsquo;s book <em>The Canon of Medicine</em> was published in the year <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1025</span> CE.</p>"},{"id":"5c841040-86b1-4722-a4cf-12a69397d8ed","content":"<p>A Persian philosopher and physician wrote a medical textbook called <em>The Canon of Medicine</em>.</p>"},{"id":"22a30a73-d7ae-4c4d-801a-7c9cd0ed7aef","content":"<p><em>The Canon of Medicine</em> was a medical textbook used by Middle Eastern and European universities for centuries.</p>"},{"id":"16753f7e-9017-44a8-81d1-636711c970c3","content":"<p>Ibn Sina recorded the most advanced medical knowledge of his time in his book <em>The Canon of Medicine</em>.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["886d631c-57af-4501-bf65-5b49e066ebcb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence identifies the year in which the book was published.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the book&rsquo;s author, it doesn&rsquo;t identify the year in which the book was published. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the book&rsquo;s historical use, it doesn&rsquo;t identify the year in which the book was published. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the book&rsquo;s contents, it doesn&rsquo;t identify the year in which the book was published.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9922b364","external_id":"37bee52c-3d2d-4773-bc56-009278a1efa6","vaultid":null,"uId":"85063e4a-62d8-4df9-83b8-4ec8a3f8ed76","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ibn Sina was a Persian philosopher and physician.</li>\n<li>His book <em>The Canon of Medicine</em> recorded the most advanced medical knowledge of his time.</li>\n<li>It was published in the year <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1025</span> CE.</li>\n<li>It was used as a medical textbook in Middle Eastern and European universities for centuries.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to identify the year that <em>The Canon of Medicine</em> was published. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"886d631c-57af-4501-bf65-5b49e066ebcb","content":"<p>Ibn Sina&rsquo;s book <em>The Canon of Medicine</em> was published in the year <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1025</span> CE.</p>"},{"id":"5c841040-86b1-4722-a4cf-12a69397d8ed","content":"<p>A Persian philosopher and physician wrote a medical textbook called <em>The Canon of Medicine</em>.</p>"},{"id":"22a30a73-d7ae-4c4d-801a-7c9cd0ed7aef","content":"<p><em>The Canon of Medicine</em> was a medical textbook used by Middle Eastern and European universities for centuries.</p>"},{"id":"16753f7e-9017-44a8-81d1-636711c970c3","content":"<p>Ibn Sina recorded the most advanced medical knowledge of his time in his book <em>The Canon of Medicine</em>.</p>"}],"keys":["886d631c-57af-4501-bf65-5b49e066ebcb"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence identifies the year in which the book was published.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the book&rsquo;s author, it doesn&rsquo;t identify the year in which the book was published. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the book&rsquo;s historical use, it doesn&rsquo;t identify the year in which the book was published. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the book&rsquo;s contents, it doesn&rsquo;t identify the year in which the book was published.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669205,"updateDate":1743430669205}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669205, 1743430669205),
    ($SATQ$9c78f702$SATQ$, $SATQ$ab69dcee-b14a-4f46-9702-9308030b492d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4858ae35-599f-49af-bbbe-3400d63d4388$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Phytoplankton play a crucial role in the ocean&rsquo;s uptake of carbon from the atmosphere. When alive, these tiny marine organisms absorb atmospheric carbon via photosynthesis. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> after they die, the phytoplankton sink to the seafloor, where the carbon in their cells gets stored in sediment, preventing it from cycling back into the atmosphere.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d305cefd-3046-4dd7-9eb4-1220b9504eb3","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"a95c8eb9-d55c-40ea-8f79-a23386ee38c4","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"92941673-54c9-4b5d-9a3d-4e40bb1fc6bb","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"2e440622-1b24-4556-8773-a7d31fd84d80","content":"<p>Then,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2e440622-1b24-4556-8773-a7d31fd84d80"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Then&rdquo; logically signals that the event described in this sentence&mdash;carbon in phytoplankton cells being trapped in sediment after the organisms have died&mdash;occurs later in a chronological sequence than the event described in the previous sentence (phytoplankton absorbing carbon while alive). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the information that follows provides specific, precise details elaborating on the previous information about what phytoplankton do when alive. Instead, this sentence explains what happens after phytoplankton die&mdash;a later step in the chronological sequence of events. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;by contrast&rdquo; illogically signals that the information that follows contrasts with the previous information about what phytoplankton do when alive. Instead, this sentence explains what happens after phytoplankton die&mdash;a later step in the chronological sequence of events. There is no contrast: in both life and death, phytoplankton contribute to the ocean&rsquo;s carbon uptake. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information that follows is in spite of the previous information about what phytoplankton do when alive. Instead, this sentence explains what happens after phytoplankton die&mdash;a later step in the chronological sequence of events. There is no contrast: in both life and death, phytoplankton contribute to the ocean&rsquo;s carbon uptake. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-270-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bcb5ee28-389d-49b5-aa0b-a23ae3076b4b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9c78f702","external_id":"ab69dcee-b14a-4f46-9702-9308030b492d","vaultid":"4858ae35-599f-49af-bbbe-3400d63d4388","uId":"ecda1266-060a-456c-99a5-59f004d3ebfc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Phytoplankton play a crucial role in the ocean&rsquo;s uptake of carbon from the atmosphere. When alive, these tiny marine organisms absorb atmospheric carbon via photosynthesis. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> after they die, the phytoplankton sink to the seafloor, where the carbon in their cells gets stored in sediment, preventing it from cycling back into the atmosphere.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d305cefd-3046-4dd7-9eb4-1220b9504eb3","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"a95c8eb9-d55c-40ea-8f79-a23386ee38c4","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"92941673-54c9-4b5d-9a3d-4e40bb1fc6bb","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"2e440622-1b24-4556-8773-a7d31fd84d80","content":"<p>Then,</p>"}],"keys":["2e440622-1b24-4556-8773-a7d31fd84d80"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Then&rdquo; logically signals that the event described in this sentence&mdash;carbon in phytoplankton cells being trapped in sediment after the organisms have died&mdash;occurs later in a chronological sequence than the event described in the previous sentence (phytoplankton absorbing carbon while alive). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the information that follows provides specific, precise details elaborating on the previous information about what phytoplankton do when alive. Instead, this sentence explains what happens after phytoplankton die&mdash;a later step in the chronological sequence of events. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;by contrast&rdquo; illogically signals that the information that follows contrasts with the previous information about what phytoplankton do when alive. Instead, this sentence explains what happens after phytoplankton die&mdash;a later step in the chronological sequence of events. There is no contrast: in both life and death, phytoplankton contribute to the ocean&rsquo;s carbon uptake. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information that follows is in spite of the previous information about what phytoplankton do when alive. Instead, this sentence explains what happens after phytoplankton die&mdash;a later step in the chronological sequence of events. There is no contrast: in both life and death, phytoplankton contribute to the ocean&rsquo;s carbon uptake. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-270-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bcb5ee28-389d-49b5-aa0b-a23ae3076b4b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959334,"updateDate":1691007959334}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959334, 1691007959334),
    ($SATQ$9d4f331c$SATQ$, $SATQ$33820d28-1cc6-4c43-8e4f-7fc2bc817315$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When sculptor Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric Auguste Bartholdi was designing the Statue of Liberty, he sought the advice of engineer Gustave Eiffel. Eiffel suggested that he make the statue&rsquo;s arm thick and position it straight above the figure&rsquo;s head. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Bartholdi decided to slim the arm and tilt it out at an angle.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"937b0963-010e-4b95-b786-ae21ce216a26","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"a6dc8af3-79f4-4090-8c4b-4ad2353f642c","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"34ee6015-5b78-466b-80cb-06e780247bd3","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"6bf3dbfc-e63a-4918-b9b9-cc2330c920ae","content":"<p>For example,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a6dc8af3-79f4-4090-8c4b-4ad2353f642c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "Instead" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that Bartholdi decided to slim and tilt out the arm of the Statue of Liberty&mdash;contrasts with Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions for the statue.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "additionally" illogically suggests that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact related to Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions in the previous sentence. Instead, it contrasts with those suggestions. Choice C is incorrect because "thus" illogically signals that the information that follows is a result of Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions in the previous sentence. Instead, it contrasts with those suggestions. Choice D is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the information about Bartholdi&rsquo;s design decisions in this sentence exemplifies Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions in the previous sentence. Instead, it contrasts with those suggestions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9d4f331c","external_id":"33820d28-1cc6-4c43-8e4f-7fc2bc817315","vaultid":null,"uId":"4c443c2a-d2da-4989-adce-00d558b90382","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When sculptor Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric Auguste Bartholdi was designing the Statue of Liberty, he sought the advice of engineer Gustave Eiffel. Eiffel suggested that he make the statue&rsquo;s arm thick and position it straight above the figure&rsquo;s head. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Bartholdi decided to slim the arm and tilt it out at an angle.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"937b0963-010e-4b95-b786-ae21ce216a26","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"a6dc8af3-79f4-4090-8c4b-4ad2353f642c","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"34ee6015-5b78-466b-80cb-06e780247bd3","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"6bf3dbfc-e63a-4918-b9b9-cc2330c920ae","content":"<p>For example,</p>"}],"keys":["a6dc8af3-79f4-4090-8c4b-4ad2353f642c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"Instead\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that Bartholdi decided to slim and tilt out the arm of the Statue of Liberty&mdash;contrasts with Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions for the statue.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"additionally\" illogically suggests that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact related to Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions in the previous sentence. Instead, it contrasts with those suggestions. Choice C is incorrect because \"thus\" illogically signals that the information that follows is a result of Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions in the previous sentence. Instead, it contrasts with those suggestions. Choice D is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the information about Bartholdi&rsquo;s design decisions in this sentence exemplifies Eiffel&rsquo;s design suggestions in the previous sentence. Instead, it contrasts with those suggestions.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323042,"updateDate":1730147323042}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323042, 1730147323042),
    ($SATQ$9dc4e640$SATQ$, $SATQ$76e2e40a-bfbf-49b0-8edb-a22d307c58c2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The mineral mtorolite is most commonly found in Zimbabwe. Mtorolite is cryptocrystalline, meaning that its crystalline structure is so fine that the individual crystals cannot be distinguished by the naked eye or even under a microscope. The crystals in microcrystalline minerals are also not visible to the naked eye; <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> they can usually be seen under a microscope.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1c9440ac-7da5-45b1-b991-ba582e41ce21","content":"<p>thus,</p>"},{"id":"a20ede72-3b93-410e-80a5-6e1ba595db32","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"8a85deb5-7205-43ac-ac14-274e263f331d","content":"<p>that said,</p>"},{"id":"d2c61585-f4b5-4269-96fc-012bf46f358d","content":"<p>similarly,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8a85deb5-7205-43ac-ac14-274e263f331d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because "that said" logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals can be seen with a microscope&mdash;is an exception to the previous information about the crystalline structure of minerals not being visible under a microscope.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "thus" illogically signals that the information that follows is a direct result or consequence of the fact that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals aren&rsquo;t visible to the naked eye. Instead, the fact that they can be seen under a microscope is an exception to the previous information about crystalline minerals. Choice B is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the information that follows exemplifies the fact that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals aren&rsquo;t visible to the naked eye. Instead, the fact that they can be seen under a microscope is an exception to the previous information about crystalline minerals. Choice D is incorrect because "similarly" illogically signals that the information that follows is similar or comparable to the fact that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals aren&rsquo;t visible to the naked eye. Instead, the fact that they can be seen under a microscope is an exception to the previous information about crystalline minerals.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9dc4e640","external_id":"76e2e40a-bfbf-49b0-8edb-a22d307c58c2","vaultid":null,"uId":"4d8345a6-43c3-4ba0-a569-4d7abd09364b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The mineral mtorolite is most commonly found in Zimbabwe. Mtorolite is cryptocrystalline, meaning that its crystalline structure is so fine that the individual crystals cannot be distinguished by the naked eye or even under a microscope. The crystals in microcrystalline minerals are also not visible to the naked eye; <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> they can usually be seen under a microscope.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1c9440ac-7da5-45b1-b991-ba582e41ce21","content":"<p>thus,</p>"},{"id":"a20ede72-3b93-410e-80a5-6e1ba595db32","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"8a85deb5-7205-43ac-ac14-274e263f331d","content":"<p>that said,</p>"},{"id":"d2c61585-f4b5-4269-96fc-012bf46f358d","content":"<p>similarly,</p>"}],"keys":["8a85deb5-7205-43ac-ac14-274e263f331d"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because \"that said\" logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals can be seen with a microscope&mdash;is an exception to the previous information about the crystalline structure of minerals not being visible under a microscope.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"thus\" illogically signals that the information that follows is a direct result or consequence of the fact that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals aren&rsquo;t visible to the naked eye. Instead, the fact that they can be seen under a microscope is an exception to the previous information about crystalline minerals. Choice B is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the information that follows exemplifies the fact that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals aren&rsquo;t visible to the naked eye. Instead, the fact that they can be seen under a microscope is an exception to the previous information about crystalline minerals. Choice D is incorrect because \"similarly\" illogically signals that the information that follows is similar or comparable to the fact that the crystals of microcrystalline minerals aren&rsquo;t visible to the naked eye. Instead, the fact that they can be seen under a microscope is an exception to the previous information about crystalline minerals.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
    ($SATQ$9e2d4ef7$SATQ$, $SATQ$de631353-acac-472f-bc92-402aa2fb57b9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$385c02a6-715a-4690-8841-124e4d958e32$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Abdulrazak Gurnah was awarded the 2021 Nobel Prize in Literature.</li>
<li>Gurnah was born in Zanzibar in East Africa and currently lives in the United Kingdom.</li>
<li>Many readers have singled out Gurnah&rsquo;s 1994 book <em>Paradise </em>for praise.</li>
<li><em>Paradise</em> is a historical novel about events that occurred in colonial East Africa.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to introduce <em>Paradise</em> to an audience unfamiliar with the novel and its author. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal? </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"48823cd0-9147-4ad0-a692-d1dd84b74745","content":"<p>Abdulrazak Gurnah, who wrote <em>Paradise </em>and later was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature, was born in Zanzibar in East Africa and currently lives in the United Kingdom.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e8ffc571-cca1-4f3a-82fa-54bc0d2122a8","content":"<p>Many readers have singled out Abdulrazak Gurnah&rsquo;s 1994 book <em>Paradise, </em>a historical novel about colonial East Africa,<em> </em>for praise.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"677d2878-732f-42d9-b42b-4158cd4546c5","content":"<p>A much-praised historical novel about colonial East Africa, <em>Paradise</em> (1994) was written by Abdulrazak Gurnah, winner of the 2021 Nobel Prize in Literature.</p>"},{"id":"25d4ad99-f8ea-4adc-8d32-b24e724e599c","content":"<p><em>Paradise</em> is a historical novel about events that occurred in colonial East Africa, Abdulrazak Gurnah&rsquo;s homeland.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["677d2878-732f-42d9-b42b-4158cd4546c5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively introduces&nbsp;<em>Paradise</em> to an audience unfamiliar with the novel and its author, describing <em>Paradise</em> as a historical novel about colonial East Africa and its author as the winner of the 2021 Nobel Prize in Literature.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence introduces Abdulrazak Gurnah to an audience unfamiliar with the author, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce <em>Paradise</em>.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about <em>Paradise</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the novel to an audience unfamiliar with its author. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about <em>Paradise</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the novel to an audience unfamiliar with its author. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-791-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1bc2f30b-1246-458f-a187-fc23dbb06cab$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9e2d4ef7","external_id":"de631353-acac-472f-bc92-402aa2fb57b9","vaultid":"385c02a6-715a-4690-8841-124e4d958e32","uId":"e5bf223f-33f8-46f0-893d-189fafc17168","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abdulrazak Gurnah was awarded the 2021 Nobel Prize in Literature.</li>\n<li>Gurnah was born in Zanzibar in East Africa and currently lives in the United Kingdom.</li>\n<li>Many readers have singled out Gurnah&rsquo;s 1994 book <em>Paradise </em>for praise.</li>\n<li><em>Paradise</em> is a historical novel about events that occurred in colonial East Africa.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to introduce <em>Paradise</em> to an audience unfamiliar with the novel and its author. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal? </p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"48823cd0-9147-4ad0-a692-d1dd84b74745","content":"<p>Abdulrazak Gurnah, who wrote <em>Paradise </em>and later was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature, was born in Zanzibar in East Africa and currently lives in the United Kingdom.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e8ffc571-cca1-4f3a-82fa-54bc0d2122a8","content":"<p>Many readers have singled out Abdulrazak Gurnah&rsquo;s 1994 book <em>Paradise, </em>a historical novel about colonial East Africa,<em> </em>for praise.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"677d2878-732f-42d9-b42b-4158cd4546c5","content":"<p>A much-praised historical novel about colonial East Africa, <em>Paradise</em> (1994) was written by Abdulrazak Gurnah, winner of the 2021 Nobel Prize in Literature.</p>"},{"id":"25d4ad99-f8ea-4adc-8d32-b24e724e599c","content":"<p><em>Paradise</em> is a historical novel about events that occurred in colonial East Africa, Abdulrazak Gurnah&rsquo;s homeland.</p>"}],"keys":["677d2878-732f-42d9-b42b-4158cd4546c5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively introduces&nbsp;<em>Paradise</em> to an audience unfamiliar with the novel and its author, describing <em>Paradise</em> as a historical novel about colonial East Africa and its author as the winner of the 2021 Nobel Prize in Literature.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence introduces Abdulrazak Gurnah to an audience unfamiliar with the author, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce <em>Paradise</em>.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about <em>Paradise</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the novel to an audience unfamiliar with its author. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about <em>Paradise</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the novel to an audience unfamiliar with its author. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-791-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1bc2f30b-1246-458f-a187-fc23dbb06cab","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$9e34720b$SATQ$, $SATQ$7ccaeef4-72d5-471c-854d-43f9b63de96b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$71e8db8d-417b-4d90-aded-a10bda7f059d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Although those who migrated to California in 1849 dreamed of finding gold nuggets in streambeds, the state&rsquo;s richest deposits were buried deeply in rock, beyond the reach of individual prospectors. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> by 1852, many had given up their fortune-hunting dreams and gone to work for one of the large companies capable of managing California&rsquo;s complex mining operations. &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3b848d54-2cc0-41a4-8f64-6f623c0053c2","content":"<p>Furthermore,</p>"},{"id":"733eb3f7-08f5-46f0-8cb7-df354876295d","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"30c8affc-a4b0-4d4a-8b4a-263bb6736cfb","content":"<p>Consequently,</p>"},{"id":"13c0a62e-52bc-4dc5-a2b5-c3d33ce535e1","content":"<p>Next,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["30c8affc-a4b0-4d4a-8b4a-263bb6736cfb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Consequently&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that many individual gold prospectors gave up their fortune-hunting dreams and became employees of mining companies&mdash;is a result or consequence of the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;furthermore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence merely adds to the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits. Instead, it&rsquo;s a result or consequence of that information. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;still&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence offers a contrast or exception to the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits. Instead, it&rsquo;s a result or consequence of that information. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;next&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is the next step in a process. Instead, it&rsquo;s a result or consequence of the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-237-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b0cd480f-bc9d-4e4d-b2d3-3a00d41f8ec3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9e34720b","external_id":"7ccaeef4-72d5-471c-854d-43f9b63de96b","vaultid":"71e8db8d-417b-4d90-aded-a10bda7f059d","uId":"b6e38d79-749a-43f4-a66c-d9c6f90a1bbd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Although those who migrated to California in 1849 dreamed of finding gold nuggets in streambeds, the state&rsquo;s richest deposits were buried deeply in rock, beyond the reach of individual prospectors. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> by 1852, many had given up their fortune-hunting dreams and gone to work for one of the large companies capable of managing California&rsquo;s complex mining operations. &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3b848d54-2cc0-41a4-8f64-6f623c0053c2","content":"<p>Furthermore,</p>"},{"id":"733eb3f7-08f5-46f0-8cb7-df354876295d","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"30c8affc-a4b0-4d4a-8b4a-263bb6736cfb","content":"<p>Consequently,</p>"},{"id":"13c0a62e-52bc-4dc5-a2b5-c3d33ce535e1","content":"<p>Next,</p>"}],"keys":["30c8affc-a4b0-4d4a-8b4a-263bb6736cfb"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Consequently&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that many individual gold prospectors gave up their fortune-hunting dreams and became employees of mining companies&mdash;is a result or consequence of the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;furthermore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence merely adds to the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits. Instead, it&rsquo;s a result or consequence of that information. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;still&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence offers a contrast or exception to the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits. Instead, it&rsquo;s a result or consequence of that information. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;next&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is the next step in a process. Instead, it&rsquo;s a result or consequence of the previous information about the inaccessibility of the state&rsquo;s gold deposits. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-237-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b0cd480f-bc9d-4e4d-b2d3-3a00d41f8ec3","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959333,"updateDate":1691007959333}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959333, 1691007959333)
